1. ControllerBase is the core MVC controller type
---------------------------------------
ControllerBase is the foundation for all MVC controllers.

It provides: HttpContext, Request, Response , RouteData , ModelState, 
             ControllerContext, Url, ObjectValidator, ModelBinderFactory, ProblemDetailsFactory

But it does NOT enforce any API-specific behavior.

It is intentionally neutral.

✔ It supports MVC
✔ It supports Web API
✔ It supports Razor Pages (indirectly)
✔ It supports custom pipelines
It is the base class for controllers, not the policy for controllers.

2. [ApiController] is NOT a controller base class — it is a behavior modifier
---------------------------------------
This is the key point.

[ApiController] is not a controller type.
It is an attribute that activates a set of API-specific conventions.

When you apply:

csharp
[ApiController]
you are telling ASP.NET Core:

“This controller is an API endpoint.
Apply API-specific rules.”

Those rules include:

✔ Automatic model validation
If ModelState.IsValid == false, MVC short-circuits and returns 400.

✔ Automatic parameter binding
Simple types come from route/query by default.

✔ Automatic 400 responses
No need to check ModelState manually.

✔ ProblemDetails formatting
Errors follow RFC 7807.

✔ Binding source inference
[FromBody], [FromQuery], [FromRoute] inferred automatically.

✔ Consistent API error responses
No HTML, no view rendering.

3. Why does ModelState validation only happen with [ApiController]?
---------------------------------------
Because model validation is not part of MVC by default.

MVC only:

Binds the model

Populates ModelState

Leaves the decision to you

Before ASP.NET Core 2.1, developers had to write:

csharp
if (!ModelState.IsValid)
    return BadRequest(ModelState);
This was error-prone and inconsistent.

So Microsoft introduced [ApiController] to enforce:

“If this is an API, treat validation failures as API errors.”

That’s why model-binding failures are tied to [ApiController], not to ControllerBase.

4. Why two hierarchies? Because they solve different problems
------------------------------------------------------------
ControllerBase = infrastructure
Provides MVC plumbing

No opinions

No automatic behavior

No API conventions

[ApiController] = policy
Adds API-specific conventions

Enforces validation

Enforces binding rules

Enforces consistent error responses

They are intentionally separate so you can choose:

MVC controllers with views → Controller

API controllers with conventions → ControllerBase + [ApiController]

API controllers without conventions → ControllerBase only

Custom pipelines → ControllerBase + your own filters
